<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Raphael</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1483">1483</a>–<a href="/years/1520">1520</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Raffaello Sanzio</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Raphael (Raffaello Sanzio) was an Italian painter and architect of the High <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a>, born in Urbino in 1483. After work in Perugia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>, he went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 1508, where he painted the frescoes of the Vatican Stanze, including The School of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, for Popes Julius II and Leo X. He also succeeded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/donato-bramante">Bramante</a> as architect of Saint Peter's Basilica. He died in Rome aged 37.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/raphael.jpg" alt="Raphael" width="320" height="436" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Raphael · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Raffaello_Sanzio.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
